WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.249
Hi, I'm Plum!

00:00:02.249 --> 00:00:06.404
A Yale researcher packaged his plotting style as a skill for coding agents.

00:00:06.404 --> 00:00:08.641
Here's how, and what its figures show.

00:00:10.225 --> 00:00:13.764
Commit each script beside its data and the figure it made, and

00:00:13.764 --> 00:00:15.828
a house style becomes checkable, not taste.

00:00:17.675 --> 00:00:21.001
An agent reads the skill file first: a router saying when to

00:00:21.001 --> 00:00:23.772
use the style, when not, and which file to open.

00:00:24.192 --> 00:00:26.552
Five reference files hold the rules.

00:00:26.552 --> 00:00:31.271
The design theory file is almost all numbers: font sizes, line widths,

00:00:31.271 --> 00:00:32.843
resolution, nine hex codes.

00:00:33.264 --> 00:00:37.370
The canonical examples are figure folders, each keeping its script, data and

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:38.738
image side by side.

00:00:39.158 --> 00:00:41.552
One file specifies nine helper functions.

00:00:41.552 --> 00:00:46.340
None exist, so each agent session writes its own, and consistency slips.

00:00:47.983 --> 00:00:49.796
Drop the top and right spines.

00:00:49.796 --> 00:00:50.702
They encode nothing.

00:00:51.122 --> 00:00:52.908
Paint your own method one dark blue.

00:00:53.831 --> 00:00:56.443
Greens for improvements, reds for baselines.

00:00:56.443 --> 00:00:59.054
Under deuteranopia, that pair nearly merges.

00:00:59.474 --> 00:01:01.177
And print the value on every bar.

00:01:03.592 --> 00:01:07.116
Here's the house style at its best, from the repository: one dark

00:01:07.116 --> 00:01:10.053
blue for the method, a red ramp ordered by rank.

00:01:10.473 --> 00:01:13.885
Every bar carries its value, and every axis starts at zero.

00:01:15.275 --> 00:01:16.520
One rule is missing.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:20.256
The headline figure starts its y-axis at zero point five, so a

00:01:20.256 --> 00:01:21.813
bar's height isn't its score.

00:01:22.233 --> 00:01:24.818
The two scores differ by one point six four times.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:26.659
Subtract the floor from both,

00:01:28.132 --> 00:01:30.163
and the drawn bars differ ten times.

00:01:30.163 --> 00:01:31.903
A bar's length is its encoding.

00:01:33.392 --> 00:01:36.667
A style guide for agents needs a checker, not a spec, and

00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:38.304
a bar's length is its encoding.

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:45.500
To recap: commit the outputs, route the agent, and make the rules a checker.

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:48.360
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:48.962
I'm Plum.

00:01:48.962 --> 00:01:49.264
Bye!
